4667 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374672 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374677 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406610 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096415 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096615 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097413 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097414 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097415 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097416 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097417 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097418 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097469 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1045 "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097413 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048436 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186096873 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ef8b70d7-53d9-5368-870b-fb3c6b4e76be" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097413 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097469 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097413 #edb/ref 1061 11889 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097413 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88224b15-9a6d-5755-a5b2-af292977df30" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097413 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097817 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097414 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097418 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097414 #edb/ref 1061 11921 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097414 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d14d2ddb-2f8f-5f82-8559-27101b7b4ca4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097414 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097818 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097415 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097417 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097415 #edb/ref 1061 11924 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097415 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3819f187-e5ca-5e24-8560-2b9695d3ec9d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097415 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096612 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097415 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097416 #edb/ref 1061 11925 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1336a15-53ea-5387-ae8a-f35eef5dcb59" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096613 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097414 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097417 #edb/ref 1061 11928 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c1dd27c-aafa-514b-8675-0cf06d6d9413" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096614 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097418 #edb/ref 1061 11929 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a827daaf-41a5-5e6d-be23-699162ae1f94" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096615 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097469 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097416 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097469 #edb/ref 1061 15506 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097469 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d77693ed-4059-5f7a-88e9-25300c5994e9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097469 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097831 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097817 #edb/ref 1064 "The trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\sin( x + {\\color{red}\\phi})\n$$\n\nis called the **amplitude-phase form** of sine. Here, ${\\color{blue}R}$ is the **amplitude** and ${\\color{red}\\phi}$ is the **phase** of the sine function.\n\nThe sum or difference of a sine function and a cosine function with the same argument can always be written as a sine function in amplitude-phase form. That is to say, if $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\sin x \\pm b \\cos x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\nR \\sin(x \\pm \\phi).\n$$\n\nfor some $R>0$ and $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097817 #edb/ref 1065 2572 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097817 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69964ee4-693c-5a43-af92-88232e3d1471" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097817 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the amplitude-phase form for sine, but there is also an amplitude-phase form for cosine. If $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\cos x \\pm b \\sin x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\cos(x \\mp {\\color{red}\\phi} )\n$$\n\nfor some ${\\color{blue}R}>0$ and ${\\color{red}\\phi} \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ \n\nNote that the sign in the last formula flips due to the identity $\\cos(x \\mp \\phi) = \\cos x \\cos \\phi \\pm \\sin x \\sin \\phi,$ which reverses the sign when expanding.\n\nWriting this more explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\operatorname{cos}x+b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ a\\operatorname{cos}x-b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's write the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sin{x}+\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form with cosine. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi.$ Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(4) \\\\  & \\approx 1.326\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}ϕ+{cos}^{2}ϕ) & =16+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =17 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =\\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\operatorname{arctan}(4)) \\\\  & \\approx \\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-1.326)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1065 2574 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3bb9db1-8b71-5f57-8cd5-5a113632e7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1067 "The Amplitude-Phase Form For Cosine" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's write the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi$. Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x} = {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{3}} \\sin \\left(x + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}} \\right)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1065 2887 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81c76ef1-c149-53cf-a9e2-d93a243a7002" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186097831 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing a Sum or Difference of Sine and Cosine Using Amplitude-Phase Form" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406589